{"reply":"Not necessarily. Different voices do not by themselves show that one self is fake and the other is the “real” one. Your confident work-self may be a practiced way of meeting demands, while your quieter family-self may emerge where less performance is required. “Act” can mean a role you’ve learned to inhabit—not a lie. Both can be genuine aspects of you, and the tension between them may be telling you something about where you feel free, watched, responsible, or understood.

That seems close to the uncertainty Alice encounters in *Advice from a Caterpillar*: she feels changed, cannot remember things as she used to, and finds that a familiar recitation comes out differently. The passage supports the experience of an unstable or shifting self-perception, but not a firm split between an authentic self and a performed one. The eligible text is limited to chapter 5, so it cannot settle the connection to your meeting and dinner note or to Hume.

I’d use Hume here less as a verdict than as a useful pressure on the question: instead of asking which voice is the “real” self, ask what continuities—and what changes—make both voices yours. Which qualities remain present in both settings, even if their volume changes?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln1004-1056","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 1004-1056","exact_quote":null}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
